// Domain vocabulary as TRANSPARENT DEFINITIONS over the parser's own lexicon (D66).
//
// ── WHY DEFINITIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// An opaque predicate — `data onco2:HasActivity : Set -> Set -> Set -> Prop { }`, a
// zero-constructor inductive — is related to nothing, so the only way to connect a parsed
// sentence to it is to ASSERT the connection: a `reflection:DeclaredResource` saying "a sentence
// of this shape warrants this predicate", on somebody's authority — an unchecked leap the kernel
// accepts because a `declared_by` field says who stood behind it.
//
// As DEFINITIONS, the connection is not asserted at all. `HasActivity(m, g, a)` unfolds to the
// parse, so the lift is definitional equality: the kernel computes it, nothing declares it.
//
// ── THE QUANTIFIER IS EXPLICIT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The sentence is «MSI cancer models had the exonuclease activity of WRN» — it says something
// about MSI cancer models, so the model is a parameter. Dropping it would turn a claim about one
// experimental context into a claim about WRN simpliciter, with the generalisation hidden in the
// predicate's ARITY, never stated, never justified.
//
// `m` is not a class IRI — the parse says «MSI cancer models», a nested compound kind, and the
// call site carries that term. That is what the sentence actually asserts.
//
// ── WHY THE BODIES DIFFER BY ONE TOKEN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `HasActivity` and `RequiresActivity` have identical bodies apart from the verb synset —
// `v02203362_t` (have) versus `v02627934_t` (require). That is not redundancy to factor out: they
// are different verbs, so they are different propositions, and a rule relating them is a claim
// about the world (see `literature-rules.esl`) rather than a definitional identity.
//
// ── WHERE THESE NAMES COME FROM (D6, settled 2026-08-10) ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// The predicate NAMES are invented — carried over from the WRN reproduction experiment
// (`experiments/publications/wrn-helicase/chain/01-onco.esl`, 2026-06-18), when the chain had no
// grounded vocabulary to draw on. `HasActivity` in particular duplicates RO:0002215 `capable of`.
//
// D6 settles that they STAY. Under `def` a name is a transparent abbreviation — the body is the
// meaning and the kernel computes it, so the name asserts nothing. And RO:0002215 is BINARY,
// gene-to-process, context-free: the explicit-model ternary form contradicts the identity its
// name would claim, so borrowing it would assert an alignment that does not hold.
//
// Grounding is capped by what the parser can see: a definition may only be written over
// vocabulary the lexicon contains, and this lexicon is WordNet + UMLS. When RO/GO enter the
// lexicon, the move is to define RO-named predicates over those entries — not to rename these.

namespace logic    = "urn:eigenius:logic";
namespace eigentt  = "urn:eigenius:eigentt";
namespace ontology = "urn:eigenius:ontology";
namespace wn       = "urn:eigenius:wn";
namespace onco2    = "urn:eigenius:demo:onco-typed";

// «⟨m⟩ had the ⟨a⟩ activity of ⟨g⟩» — the parse of sentence 1, abstracted over its three content
// positions. Unfolds to exactly the term `claim_1` carries; verified by
// `the_definition_unfolds_to_exactly_the_committed_parse` in eigenius-reasoning's tests.
//
// Reading the body outward: the subject is THE unique thing that is an `a`-kind and is `of` the
// kind `g` (`the(…)` picks it, `eigentt:fst` projects the witness out of the Σ); the object is the
// kind `m`. `wn:n13440063` is the synset the activity noun sits in.
def onco2:HasActivity(m : Set, g : Set, a : Set) : Prop =
    wn:v02203362_t(
        eigentt:fst(ontology:the(
            (exists x0 : wn:n13440063 =>
                logic:And(
                    ontology:compound_kind(x0, a),
                    ontology:prep_of(x0, ontology:kind_of(g))
                )))),
        ontology:kind_of(m));

// «⟨m⟩ required the ⟨a⟩ activity of ⟨g⟩» — sentence 2. Identical to the above but for the verb.
def onco2:RequiresActivity(m : Set, g : Set, a : Set) : Prop =
    wn:v02627934_t(
        eigentt:fst(ontology:the(
            (exists x0 : wn:n13440063 =>
                logic:And(
                    ontology:compound_kind(x0, a),
                    ontology:prep_of(x0, ontology:kind_of(g))
                )))),
        ontology:kind_of(m));
